#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065341 :tutorial/content "A **perfect square trinomial** is a three-term expression that is the result of squaring a binomial.\n\nFor example, the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + 9\n$$\n\nis a perfect square trinomial because it is the result of squaring the binomial $(x+3)$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial, we have to figure out what binomial it is the square of. We can often do this by relating the perfect square trinomial to the **square of a sum**:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2+6x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the \"square of a sum formula\". In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{3}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nThis method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. We will learn more general methods in the future." :tutorial/id #uuid "c04b9f03-f29f-5feb-8d13-7de9fc378a34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1596 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065345 :tutorial/content "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the **square of a difference**:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x  + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2-10x+25$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{5}},$ and we get\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n  $$" :tutorial/id #uuid "70396264-e3c3-50d9-94a4-95e8017dbfde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1855 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"}]}
